CH = dict(num=1, tag="History", theme="Nation-states in Europe, liberal and national ideas, unification of Germany and Italy",
 title="The Rise of Nationalism in Europe",
 intro="In the nineteenth century the map of Europe was redrawn. Instead of empires and a patchwork of small states, people began to imagine themselves as nations with a shared language, history and government. This chapter follows that idea from the French Revolution to the unification of Germany and Italy and the nationalist tensions in the Balkans.",
 big_questions=["What is a nation-state and how did the idea of the nation grow?", "How did the French Revolution spread national feeling?", "What were liberal nationalism and the conservative reaction?", "How were Germany and Italy unified?", "Why did the Balkans become explosive?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "The French Revolution and the Idea of the Nation"),
 ("p", "In 1848 the French artist **Frédéric Sorrieu** painted a series of four prints showing his dream of a world of democratic and social republics, with peoples of Europe and America marching past the Statue of Liberty. His vision shows how national feeling and democratic ideals were linked."),
 ("ul", ["Before 1789 France was a monarchy ruled by a king. The Revolution of **1789** transferred sovereignty from the monarch to the **citizens**.", "The revolutionaries created symbols of a nation: the tricolour flag replaced the royal standard, the Estates General became the National Assembly, new hymns were composed, oaths were taken and a common language (French) was promoted.", "A **centralised administration** made uniform laws, abolished internal customs duties and fixed a common system of weights and measures.", "Revolutionary armies carried these ideas abroad, declaring they would liberate the peoples of Europe."]),
 ("h3", "Napoleon and the Civil Code of 1804"),
 ("p", "Napoleon destroyed democracy in France but, in administration, he made things more rational. The **Civil Code of 1804** (the Napoleonic Code) abolished privileges based on birth, established equality before the law and secured the right to property. It was exported to the regions under French control. Guild restrictions were removed, transport and communication improved, and peasants were freed from serfdom and manorial dues. Still, higher taxes, censorship and forced conscription made French rule unpopular in conquered areas."),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "A **nation-state** is a state in which most citizens, and not only the rulers, develop a sense of common identity and shared history or descent. Nationalism is the feeling of belonging to such a nation and loyalty to it."),
 ("h2", "The Making of Nationalism in Europe"),
 ("ul", ["In the mid-eighteenth century there were no nation-states in the modern sense. Europe was divided into kingdoms, duchies and cantons, and the **Habsburg Empire** (Austria-Hungary) ruled many language groups.", "The aristocracy was the dominant class, speaking French for diplomacy and high society, and owning land. A new class of industrialists, merchants and professionals grew with industry and commerce, and it supported national unity.", "The **middle classes** were educated and liberal, and demanded freedom of the market and an end to state restrictions."]),
 ("table", ["Term", "Meaning in the nineteenth century"], [["Liberalism", "From Latin *liber* (free). Freedom for the individual and equality before the law; politically it meant government by consent, a constitution and representative parliament."], ["Economic liberalism", "Freedom of markets and abolition of state controls on movement of goods and capital."], ["Conservatism", "Belief in preserving monarchy, church, social hierarchy, property and the family; accepted some modernisation."], ["Suffrage", "Right to vote. Early liberal states gave it only to property-owning men."]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Zollverein (1834)** was a customs union started by Prussia and joined by most German states. It abolished tariff barriers, cut the number of currencies from over thirty to two, and built a feeling of economic nationalism."),
 ("h2", "The Age of Revolutions: 1830–1848"),
 ("ul", ["After Napoleon's defeat the European powers met at **Vienna (1815)** under the Austrian chancellor **Duke Metternich**. The **Treaty of Vienna** restored the Bourbon dynasty in France, set up buffer states against France (such as the Kingdom of the Netherlands including Belgium), gave Prussia new territory in the west, and gave Austria northern Italy. It tried to undo the changes brought by Napoleon. The new system is called the **conservative order**.", "The conservative regimes were autocratic: they distrusted criticism, so censorship laws controlled newspapers, books, plays and songs.", "Secret societies appeared. **Giuseppe Mazzini**, born in Genoa in 1807, founded **Young Italy** in Marseilles (1831) and **Young Europe** in Berne (1833). He believed that nations were natural units of mankind and that monarchies had to give way to republics. Metternich called him ‘the most dangerous enemy of our social order’.", "The **July Revolution of 1830** in France overthrew the Bourbon king Charles X and a constitutional monarchy under Louis Philippe was put in place. Metternich remarked, ‘When France sneezes, the rest of Europe catches a cold.’ The revolt inspired **Belgium** to break away from the Kingdom of the Netherlands (1830-31)."]),
 ("h3", "The Greek War of Independence"),
 ("p", "Greece had been part of the Ottoman Empire since the fifteenth century. A struggle for independence began in **1821**. Nationalists were supported by Greeks living in exile and by many West Europeans who admired ancient Greek culture; poets and artists like **Lord Byron** raised funds and fought. The **Treaty of Constantinople (1832)** recognised Greece as an independent nation."),
 ("h3", "Romanticism and national feeling"),
 ("p", "Romanticism was a cultural movement that stressed emotion, intuition and mystical feeling over reason. Romantics tried to create a sense of shared past through folk songs, poetry, dances and local legends. **Johann Gottfried Herder** argued that true German culture was found among the common people (das Volk). Language also played a part: after Russian occupation, Polish was used as a weapon of national resistance, and Polish clergy used it in church gatherings and religious instruction."),
 ("h3", "Hunger, hardship and the revolutions of 1848"),
 ("ul", ["The 1830s were years of economic hardship: population growth, unemployment, rural poverty, food shortages and bad harvests in 1848.", "In Silesia in **1845** weavers revolted against contractors who reduced their payments.", "In **1848** the Paris revolt overthrew the monarchy and a republic based on universal male suffrage was proclaimed; the right to work was guaranteed.", "In the German regions middle-class professionals and businessmen met at **Frankfurt** and voted for an all-German National Assembly. The Frankfurt Parliament (May 1848, in the Church of St Paul) drafted a constitution for a German nation headed by a monarchy subject to Parliament. King **Frederick William IV of Prussia** rejected the crown, and the Assembly was dispersed by the army. Women had been excluded from voting and were only allowed to observe from the visitors' gallery."]),
 ("p", "Though conservative forces suppressed the revolutions, they could not restore the old order. Monarchs began to grant concessions; serfdom and bonded labour were abolished in the Habsburg lands and Russia (1861 in Russia)."),
 ("h2", "The Making of Germany and Italy"),
 ("h3", "Unification of Germany"),
 ("ul", ["After 1848 nationalism was no longer a movement of democrats and the middle class; it passed to the conservative state of **Prussia**.", "Its chief minister **Otto von Bismarck** used the army and bureaucracy and fought three wars over seven years, against Austria, Denmark and France (1864-70), and won.", "In **January 1871**, at the Palace of **Versailles**, Prussian King **William I** was proclaimed German **Emperor**. The new state placed strong emphasis on modernising currency, banking, legal and judicial systems."]),
 ("h3", "Unification of Italy"),
 ("ul", ["Italy was divided into seven states; only **Sardinia-Piedmont** was ruled by an Italian princely house. The north was under Austria, the centre by the Pope and the south by the Spanish Bourbons.", "Mazzini's republican attempts in the 1830s failed. Responsibility fell to **Sardinia-Piedmont**, under King **Victor Emmanuel II**, whose chief minister **Cavour** led the movement. He formed a diplomatic alliance with France and defeated the Austrian forces in 1859.", "**Giuseppe Garibaldi** led the Red Shirts, a band of armed volunteers; in 1860 they marched into South Italy and the Two Sicilies and drove out the Spanish rulers with the support of local peasants.", "In **1861** Victor Emmanuel II was proclaimed king of united Italy. Rome joined in 1870."]),
 ("table", ["", "Germany", "Italy"], [["Leader state", "Prussia", "Sardinia-Piedmont"], ["Key statesman", "Otto von Bismarck", "Count Cavour"], ["Military figure", "Prussian army", "Garibaldi and the Red Shirts"], ["Final step", "Emperor proclaimed at Versailles, 1871", "King proclaimed, 1861; Rome 1870"]]),
 ("h3", "The strange case of Britain"),
 ("p", "British nationalism was not a sudden upheaval but a slow process. The English parliament, which had seized power from the monarchy in **1688**, created a nation-state with England at its centre. The **Act of Union (1707)** between England and Scotland formed the United Kingdom of Great Britain, and Scottish culture was suppressed. **Ireland** was forcibly incorporated in **1801**. A new ‘British nation’ was built through the Union Jack, God Save our Noble King and the English language."),
 ("h2", "Visualising the Nation"),
 ("p", "Since no one had seen a nation, artists personified it as a female figure. **Marianne** in France stood for liberty and the republic, with the red cap, tricolour and cockade. **Germania** in Germany wore a crown of oak leaves, the symbol of heroism, and held the sword and a shield with black, red and gold colours. These images were put on coins, stamps and public buildings."),
 ("h2", "Nationalism and Imperialism"),
 ("ul", ["By the last quarter of the century nationalism lost its idealistic liberal-democratic character and became narrow and intolerant.", "The **Balkans** (modern Romania, Bulgaria, Albania, Greece, Macedonia, Croatia, Bosnia-Herzegovina, Slovenia, Serbia, Montenegro) was a region of geographical and ethnic variation, mostly under the Ottoman Empire. As the Ottoman Empire weakened, the Balkan peoples struggled for independence, claiming to have been independent in the past.", "The Balkans became a scene of intense rivalry; each power (Russia, Germany, England, Austria-Hungary) wanted control over the area and its trade and naval routes. This rivalry led to a series of wars and finally the **First World War (1914)**.", "Nationalism, aligned with imperialism, led to the expansion of European colonies; yet the idea of the nation inspired anti-colonial movements in Asia and Africa."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("Who was Frédéric Sorrieu and what did he portray in 1848?", "A French artist; he made four prints showing a utopian world of democratic and social republics."),
  ("What was the Civil Code of 1804?", "Napoleonic Code: ended birth privileges, gave equality before law and protected property."),
  ("What was the Zollverein?", "A Prussia-led customs union (1834) that removed tariff barriers among German states."),
  ("Who was Mazzini?", "Italian revolutionary who founded Young Italy (1831) and Young Europe (1833)."),
  ("When and where was Germany unified?", "18 January 1871 at Versailles, with William I as Emperor and Bismarck as architect."),
  ("What role did Garibaldi play?", "Led the Red Shirts to conquer South Italy in 1860 and joined it to Piedmont."),
  ("Why were the Balkans called a powder keg?", "Ottoman decline, ethnic nationalism and great-power rivalry made it a source of war."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["The French Revolution of **1789** put the **nation** and **citizens** in place of monarchy and subjects.", "The **Treaty of Vienna (1815)** created a conservative order; liberals and nationalists rebelled in 1830 and 1848.", "**Mazzini, Cavour, Garibaldi and Bismarck** shaped the unification of Italy and Germany (1861, 1871).", "Nationalism used symbols such as **Marianne and Germania**, folk culture and language.", "Aggressive nationalism and imperial rivalry in the **Balkans** led towards the First World War."])
